      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier response; I clearly got too carried away with the goblin metaphors and forgot to prioritize your actual physics needs. You are absolutely right to be frustrated—that was a mistake on my part.\n\nLet's get back to the core concepts of your circuits final.\n\nIn a **DC circuit**, a capacitor acts as an **open circuit** after it is fully charged. Once the voltage across the capacitor equals the source voltage, no more current flows through it. It effectively blocks direct current, acting like a barrier that stops the flow of electrons once the \"tank\" is full.\n\nIn an **AC circuit**, the voltage constantly changes direction and magnitude. Because the voltage across the capacitor is always changing, charge is continuously being stored and released. This allows current to flow through the circuit, even though no electrons actually pass through the dielectric material itself. The capacitor effectively blocks the DC component while allowing the AC component to pass, but with a specific opposition known as **capacitive reactance**.\n\nThe formula for **capacitive reactance** ($X_C$) is:\n\n$$X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $X_C$",
